## Cybersecurity Starts with a Well-Trained Workforce

Libraries rely on technology to serve patrons, process payments, and keep sensitive information. Firewalls, antivirus software, and other technical safeguards are important, but the people using those systems remain essential to protecting them.

The Comptroller's office recently made the same point. Cybersecurity awareness training helps staff and officials recognize phishing emails, malicious attachments, and social engineering attempts, and it helps them understand their own responsibility for protecting information. Good training also reinforces an organization's IT policies and gives people practical steps to take when something goes wrong.

The Comptroller has found places where this was missing. Audits of [a village](https://www.osc.ny.gov/local-government/audits/village/2026/08/07/village-lacona-cybersecurity-2026m-23) and [a charter school](https://www.osc.ny.gov/local-government/audits/charter-school/2026/04/10/roosevelt-childrens-academy-charter-school-information-technology-2025m) found that employees did not receive adequate cybersecurity awareness training. That raises the risk that sensitive information could be shared by accident and that systems could be compromised.

Libraries are not exempt. The Comptroller can audit any library on its cybersecurity practices.

### Keeping It Going

Threats keep changing, so awareness is an ongoing responsibility and not a one-time course. Periodic refreshers and regular reminders of good habits make a simple mistake less likely to become a costly incident. New staff, volunteers, and trustees should be trained as they join.

To learn more, see the Comptroller's *Information Technology Governance* Local Government Management Guide and its other cybersecurity resources in the [Local Government Publications](https://www.osc.ny.gov/local-government/publications) section of its website.

