## This Week in OSC Audits

Below is a summary of recent Office of the State Comptroller audits. These audits examine a charter school, a town, a village, and a school district. The settings vary, but the themes are familiar ones for any public institution, including libraries.

### [A Charter School: Information Technology Asset Management](https://www.osc.ny.gov/local-government/audits/charter-school/2026/10/23/academy-health-sciences-charter-school-information-technology-asset)

Auditors found that a charter school's board had not adopted an IT asset inventory policy. The inventory of about 1,200 assets was incomplete and inaccurate, and it lacked details such as serial numbers, location, assigned employee, and cost. Of 45 assets tested, 13 costing about $4,020, including Chromebooks and laptops, could not be located. Officials said they did periodic physical counts but could not document them.

**Takeaways for libraries**

- A short inventory policy that sets what gets tracked, who tracks it, and how often it is counted gives the board something to measure against.
- Record the serial number, location, assigned user, and cost for each device.
- Document every physical count, because a count that cannot be shown did not happen as far as an auditor is concerned.

### [A Town: Town Clerk/Tax Collector](https://www.osc.ny.gov/local-government/audits/town/2026/10/23/town-hermon-town-clerktax-collector-2026m-93)

Auditors found that a town clerk, who collected about $1.6 million in 2025, did not properly record and deposit all collections, and the records could not show that cash was deposited intact and on time. Of $587,148 in collections tested, $197 less cash was deposited than was recorded. No monthly bank reconciliations or accountability analyses were done, and the analysis auditors performed showed an unexplained cash surplus of $923. Officials agreed with the five recommendations.

**Takeaways for libraries**

- Deposit cash intact and promptly, and compare each deposit to the receipts recorded.
- Reconcile bank accounts to the books every month, and have someone other than the person handling the cash review the reconciliation.
- Small differences still deserve an explanation, because they show whether the controls work.

### [A Village: Treasurer's Duties and Claims Audit](https://www.osc.ny.gov/local-government/audits/village/2026/10/23/village-brightwaters-treasurers-duties-and-claims-audit-2026m-84)

Auditors found that a village board did not separate the treasurer's duties, did not review bank reconciliations, and did not control signature stamps. Of 183 disbursements reviewed, totaling $1.3 million, 109 totaling $381,067 were not audited before payment. In addition, 29 claims ($72,557) lacked support, 22 claims ($81,504) lacked required quotes, and three claims improperly included sales tax. Officials agreed with the nine recommendations.

**Takeaways for libraries**

- Audit every claim before payment, and require supporting documentation for each one.
- Control who has access to signature stamps, check stock, and the software used to print checks.
- Follow the procurement policy and keep the required quotes on file.
- Have the board review bank reconciliations, especially when one person handles most of the financial work.

### [A School District: Audit Follow-Up](https://www.osc.ny.gov/local-government/audits/school-district/2026/10/30/wheelerville-union-free-school-district-audit-follow-2022m-2-f)

A 2022 audit found that a school district did not keep IT asset inventory records and stored unused equipment in unsecured areas. This follow-up found that officials fully implemented one of the six recommendations, which was to physically safeguard unused IT assets. The other five, covering policies, complete inventory records, periodic physical inventories, and proper disposal, were not fully implemented.

**Takeaways for libraries**

- Track each audit recommendation to completion, and have the board review progress on a regular schedule.
- Assign a specific person and date to each corrective action.
- Include unused and retired equipment in the inventory until it is properly disposed of.

> Across all of these audits, a consistent pattern appears. Problems rarely begin with fraud or misconduct. They begin with small gaps in oversight, unclear documentation, or policies that drift out of alignment with actual practice. Regular review of financial procedures, procurement policies, and board reporting practices helps prevent those small gaps from becoming larger governance failures.
